  "summary": "Sparse-label fine-tuning, generative chemical-space access, and LLM agents are being benchmarked against conventional virtual screening.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational prioritization of therapeutic candidates, whether small molecules, drug combinations, or herbal formulations, using learned models over structure, activity, or network data. Boltz-2 affinity heads fine-tuned on 40-300 binary activity labels improve early enrichment on eight MF-PCBA targets, with 300 labels yielding 1.77-fold top-1% active enrichment and 2.14-fold AP. Generative REAL-SWIT learns Enamine REAL Space to support docking-guided ultra-large screening, and a closed-loop LLM agent is benchmarked for multi-objective hit-to-lead design across six kinase targets. Network-based work applies similar logic to repurposing: LLM-mined PubMed evidence ranks drug pairs for ER+ breast cancer, while multilayer integration, including MR and snRNA-seq, prioritizes RAF-MEK-ERK/MAPK3 in depression. Network pharmacology with linear SVM and GCN ranks Korean herbal formulations for hypertension. The remaining items are reviews of AI-guided platforms rather than primary results.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
